# Shell scripts get executed inside Linux containers or by /bin/sh directly — a Windows
# checkout that rewrites their line endings to CRLF breaks them (Alpine's ash reads a line like
# "set -e\r" as the illegal option "-e<CR>" and exits). `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree
# on every platform, overriding whatever core.autocrlf a contributor's git is set to — the
# infra/crcon-db-entrypoint.sh incident this fixes did not require the user to do anything wrong,
# just the out-of-the-box Windows git default.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files go the other way: cmd.exe expects CRLF line endings, and with LF-only lines `goto`
# and `call` can miss a label that is plainly there (a long-standing cmd.exe quirk). A ZIP download
# from GitHub, or a checkout with autocrlf off, gave exactly that copy; `eol=crlf` writes CRLF into
# the working tree on every platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf
# install.bat is the one people download on its own, and a single-file download from GitHub is the
# file as stored, which for the rule above is LF. So it is stored with its CRLF as it is.
install.bat -text
